    "result_text": "BUILD SUCCESS \u2014 all tests green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented duplicate-email rejection (409) for owner creation by mirroring the existing `EnsureUniqueTelephone` pattern, keeping structural impact minimal \u2014 each new unit is a small, single-method class with no surrounding complexity, and I only *edited* one existing file (the YAML pipeline, not a function).\n\n**New files:**\n- `EnsureUniqueEmail.java` \u2014 a step function that reads the built `Owner` via `@Val`, scans `OwnerRepository.findAll()`, and throws `DuplicateEmailException` on a match. Emails are already lower-cased at the boundary by `Owner.setEmail`, so a plain `equals` gives the required case-insensitive check; a null email is skipped.\n- `DuplicateEmailException.java` \u2014 checked exception (extends `Exception` so it appears in the `throws` clause).\n- `DuplicateEmailExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 responds `409 CONFLICT` with a `ProblemDetail`, matching the telephone handler.\n- `...DuplicateEmailException.yml` \u2014 escalation mapping.\n\n**Edited:**\n- `owners.POST.yml` \u2014 inserted an `ensureUniqueEmail` step between the telephone-uniqueness and household steps (before `save`), so a conflict is a 409 rather than a persisted duplicate, and it shares the same `transaction` governance as its neighbours.\n\nNo existing function or class was grown \u2014 the only functions I authored each have CC 1\u20132 and zero sibling methods (WMC_other = 0).",
